<h2 id="apps-heading">CDLR Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Pallet conveyor systems:</strong> CDLR is the default drive technology for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyor systems</a>. Loaded pallets (typically 1,500-3,500 lbs) transport between palletizers, stretch wrappers, labelers, and shipping docks on CDLR conveyors. Right-angle chain <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> route pallets to multiple shipping lanes.</p>
<p><strong>Drum and barrel handling:</strong> Steel drums and barrels (500-2,000 lbs) ride on CDLR rollers. The positive chain drive prevents the slip issues that belt-driven systems experience with round, smooth drum surfaces. Chemical, petroleum, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a> facilities use CDLR for drum transport.</p>
<p><strong>Heavy case transport:</strong> Cases and totes exceeding 200 lbs, such as automotive parts, appliance components, and heavy industrial goods, use CDLR conveyors. The positive chain drive handles variable loads without the speed variation that belt-driven systems exhibit under changing load conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Between palletizer and stretch wrapper:</strong> The section between the palletizer discharge and the stretch wrapper infeed is one of the most critical CDLR applications in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>. Full pallets (often 2,000-3,500 lbs) must transfer reliably at 20-40 FPM with precise positioning for the stretch wrapper's turntable or ring wrapper. CDLR provides the torque and positive drive this application demands.</p>
<p><strong>With <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors</a>:</strong> CDLR sections feed loaded pallets onto VRC platforms for multi-level transport. The positive chain drive ensures reliable loading/unloading of the VRC even with heavy, off-center pallet loads.</p>
<h2 id="maintenance-heading">CDLR Maintenance</h2>
<p>CDLR conveyors require more maintenance than <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> but handle loads that MDR cannot. Key maintenance items include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chain lubrication:</strong> Apply chain lubricant monthly for standard-duty applications, weekly for heavy continuous duty. Use food-grade lubricant (H1) in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage</a> environments. Under-lubricated chains wear 3-5x faster.</li>
<li><strong>Chain tension:</strong> Check chain tension quarterly. Loose chains skip sprocket teeth and cause roller speed variation. Over-tight chains accelerate bearing and sprocket wear.</li>
<li><strong>Sprocket inspection:</strong> Inspect sprocket teeth for hooked, worn, or broken profiles annually. Replace sprockets when tooth wear reaches 50% of original tooth thickness.</li>
<li><strong>Chain replacement:</strong> Replace chains when stretch exceeds 3% of original length. Properly lubricated ANSI roller chains last 3-5 years in standard pallet handling. Replace all chains in a section simultaneously to maintain uniform speed.</li>
<li><strong>Bearing and roller inspection:</strong> Check for rough-running, seized, or noisy bearings semi-annually. Replace failed rollers individually without dismantling the chain drive.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What does CDLR stand for?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>CDLR stands for Chain Driven Live Roller. Roller chains and sprockets positively drive all rollers with zero slip. CDLR is the standard for heavy-duty <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveying</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the weight capacity of a CDLR conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Standard: 500-4,000 lbs. Heavy-duty structural: up to 10,000+ lbs. Capacity depends on roller diameter, wall thickness, bearing type, frame gauge, and chain size.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">When should I use CDLR instead of MDR?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Use CDLR when product weight exceeds 250-500 lbs, you need zero-slip positive drive, or product bottoms have poor friction. Use <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> for lighter loads needing zone control and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What maintenance does CDLR require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Monthly chain lubrication, quarterly chain tension checks, annual sprocket inspection, and chain replacement every 3-5 years (when stretch exceeds 3%). Motor/gearbox lubrication per manufacturer schedule.</p>

</div>
